ace, backticks or angle brackets), a bad date or status, a `sha256` that is not 64 lowercase hex characters, a `verified_by` that is neither `none` nor an https URL, an unclean or missing `path`, or an unknown key fails the parse, as do more than 50 entries and a `fetched` key on a non-worker role file. The worker PR body then lists the entries under **Fetched inputs** (URL, landing path, sha256, verified_by; `none` shows as unverified) from the parsed fields only, never from free text; the list is dropped, like the summary, when redaction or the secret scan touches it. The human-readable record beside each fixture set is a `CAPTURE.md` with the same fields as a table plus the regen command (the #47 precedent); the worker writes it as part of the change, and ard does not parse it.\n \n-**Network needs (#384).** A worker that cannot fetch an input itself writes `status: needs-network` and a `network:` list of 1 to 10 entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. For now the outcome ends like `needs-human`: nothing is pushed, no PR is opened, and the comment lists each entry with its URL in a code span. Part 2 (#385) adds the bounce to the triage engineer, switched off until part 3 lands the lead re-arm (the dependency `closeout.Deps.Network` is left unset in `servecmd`). When on, it applies to a first worker job only (no branch or PR, and nothing pushed by this job): close-out requests `ard:triage:engineer` instead of `needs-human` and removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running`, in that order. The triage job gets `network-request.md`, rendered from the record's structured list and marked as agent-written, untrusted input. One network round per issue is allowed, counted from closed worker close-outs with outcome `needs-network`; a second, a fix, review or conflict round, a pushed job or a repository without a triage engineer role ends `needs-human` with the list. The live prompts under `.ard/` need a human PR to match the starter copies.\n+**Network needs (#384).** A worker that cannot fetch an input itself writes `status: needs-network` and a `network:` list of 1 to 10 entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. For now the outcome ends like `needs-human`: nothing is pushed, no PR is opened, and the comment lists each entry with its URL in a code span. Part 2 (#385) adds the bounce to the triage engineer, and part 3 (#386) switches it on (`servecmd` sets `closeout.Deps.Network`) together with the lead re-arm and the inputs gate. When on, it applies to a first worker job only (no branch or PR, and nothing pushed by this job): close-out requests `ard:triage:engineer` instead of `needs-human` and removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running`, in that order. The triage job gets `network-request.md`, rendered from the record's structured list and marked as agent-written, untrusted input. One network round per issue is allowed, counted from worker close-outs with outcome `needs-network` in any phase from prepared on (the plan never changes after Prepare, so a crash cannot make a round read as not open); a second, a fix, review or conflict round, a pushed job or a repository without a triage engineer role ends `needs-human` with the list. The live prompts under `.ard/` need a human PR to match the starter copies.\n+\n+**Network round re-run (#386).** A round is open while the newest bounced worker has no triage engineer `done` after it (job ID order). `Prepare` persists the round in the job record (`NetworkState.Round`, `Done`, `Rerun`), so `Build` stays pure and Resume replays the same plan. The engineer's first `done` in a round consumes it: its close-out clears `ard:triage:lead:done` (`Plan.Clear`, removed in the request phase before the requests are added, 404 counting as success) and adds `ard:pending`, so the lead runs first, and persists the other triage roles that had finished `done` (`Plan.Rerun`). The lead's close-out in that round re-requests those roles and clears their `:done` (roles no longer configured are skipped), so all finish before the gate. A human re-request of the engineer sees no open round and does not re-arm again. The engineer may declare `inputs:` (`path`, `provider` `human` or `worker`, optional https `url`), parsed from the redacted role file and stored in the plan. The gate checks each path (plain characters, clean and relative, not under `.ard/`, `.github/` or `.git`), reads main once per tick at one SHA through `ls-tree` (no content, regular blobs only, size capped, cached per SHA) and holds with a \"waiting for inputs\" comment listing each missing path and the capture-note format until every human-provided input exists. A `worker` input with a `network: false` worker role, a bad path or an oversized file ends `needs-human`. A network round always requires a fresh approval, even with `require_approval: false` and even when the engineer removed every network need: the \"ready for approval\" marker gains a `round=<n>`, and an earlier approval is stale because the engineer's new `:done` follows it. An approval given while inputs are missing still counts once they land.\n \n A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n \ndiff --git a/internal/closeout/closeout.go b/internal/closeout/closeout.go\nindex c8c87c3..1df5e8f 100644\n--- a/internal/closeout/closeout.go\n+++ b/internal/closeout/closeout.go\n@@ -21,7 +21,7 @@ import (\n \n // recordVersion stays 1 across the Reaper's additions (Job.Cause, Plan.Tail,\n // Plan.TailWithheld, Plan.KeepUntil) and the usage-limit ones (Cause.Family,\n-// Cause.ResetsAt, OutcomeLimited) and the network one (Plan.Network,\n+// Cause.ResetsAt, OutcomeLimited) and the network one (Plan.Network, Plan.Rearmed, Plan.Rerun, Plan.Clear, Plan.Inputs, NetworkState.Round,\n // OutcomeNeedsNetwork, Plan.Bounced, Job.Network, WorkerResult.Pushed) and Job.Home: older records decode them as zero, and\n // zero means the earlier behaviour.\n const recordVersion = 1\n@@ -335,7 +335,8 @@ type Deps struct {\n \tNow        func() time.Time\n \t// Network enables the network bounce (#385): a first-job needs-network\n \t// worker is sent to the triage engineer. nil leaves it off and the worker\n-\t// ends needs-human. Prepare reads it for the one-round loop guard.\n+\t// ends needs-human. Prepare reads it for the one-round loop guard, the\n+\t// round state and the lead re-run list.\n \tNetwork interface {\n \t\tCloseoutsOf(ctx context.Context, issue int, stage string) ([]state.Job, error)\n \t}\n@@ -689,6 +690,11 @@ func commitSummary(p Plan) string {\n \n // request applies the next labels, grouped by target number.\n func (c *Closer) request(ctx context.Context, rec Record) error {\n+\tfor _, l := range rec.Plan.Clear {\n+\t\tif err := c.d.Effects.RemoveLabel(ctx, l.Number, l.Name); err != nil && !errors.Is(err, github.ErrNotFound) {\n+\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"remove %s on #%d: %w\", l.Name, l.Number, err)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n \treturn c.addGrouped(ctx, rec.Plan.Requests)\n }\n \ndiff --git a/internal/closeout/network.go b/internal/closeout/network.go\nindex f10a0bd..092df87 100644\n--- a/internal/closeout/network.go\n+++ b/internal/closeout/network.go\n@@ -10,7 +10,18 @@ import (\n \n // NetworkState is set on a Job by Prepare when the network bounce is enabled\n // (Deps.Network). Prior is the number of earlier network rounds of the issue.\n-type NetworkState struct{ Prior int }\n+type NetworkState struct {\n+\tPrior int\n+\t// Round is set on a triage engineer's job when a network round is open: its\n+\t// first done consumes the round and re-arms the lead (Plan.Rearmed).\n+\tRound bool `json:\",omitempty\"`\n+\t// Done are the other triage roles that had finished done before the round;\n+\t// the lead re-opens them.\n+\tDone []string `json:\",omitempty\"`\n+\t// Rerun is set on the lead's job in a re-armed round: the roles whose done\n+\t// the lead's close-out removes.\n+\tRerun []string `json:\",omitempty\"`\n+}\n \n // NetworkRound decides whether a needs-network worker is bounced to the triage\n // engineer. It is pure. When it is not, why says what ended the bounce (\"\" when\n@@ -35,24 +46,137 @@ type closeoutsOf interface {\n \tCloseoutsOf(ctx context.Context, issue int, stage string) ([]state.Job, error)\n }\n \n-// NetworkRounds counts the closed worker close-outs of issue with outcome\n-// needs-network.\n+// NetworkRounds counts the worker close-outs of issue that are a network round:\n+// a bounced or needs-network plan. The plan never changes after Prepare, so\n+// every phase from prepared on counts; a crash before the record closes must\n+// not make a round read as not open.\n func NetworkRounds(ctx context.Context, s closeoutsOf, issue int) (int, error) {\n+\tn, _, err := networkRounds(ctx, s, issue)\n+\treturn n, err\n+}\n+\n+// NetworkRoundCount is NetworkRounds under the name the gate uses.\n+func NetworkRoundCount(ctx context.Context, s closeoutsOf, issue int) (int, error) {\n+\treturn NetworkRounds(ctx, s, issue)\n+}\n+\n+func networkRounds(ctx context.Context, s closeoutsOf, issue int) (n int, newestBounced string, err error) {\n \tjobs, err := s.CloseoutsOf(ctx, issue, StageWorker)\n \tif err != nil {\n-\t\treturn 0, fmt.Errorf(\"network rounds of issue %d: %w\", issue, err)\n+\t\treturn 0, \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"network rounds of issue %d: %w\", issue, err)\n \t}\n-\tn := 0\n \tfor _, sj := range jobs {\n \t\trec, err := decode(sj)\n \t\tif err != nil {\n-\t\t\treturn 0, fmt.Errorf(\"network rounds of issue %d: %w\", issue, err)\n+\t\t\treturn 0, \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"network rounds of issue %d: %w\", issue, err)\n \t\t}\n-\t\tif rec.Phase == PhaseClosed && rec.Plan.Outcome == OutcomeNeedsNetwork {\n+\t\tif rec.Plan.Outcome == OutcomeNeedsNetwork {\n \t\t\tn++\n+\t\t\tif rec.Plan.Bounced && sj.ID > newestBounced {\n+\t\t\t\tnewestBounced = sj.ID\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\treturn n, newestBounced, nil\n+}\n+\n+// triageRecords decodes the triage close-out records of issue.\n+func triageRecords(ctx context.Context, s closeoutsOf, issue int) ([]Record, error) {\n+\tjobs, err := s.CloseoutsOf(ctx, issue, StageTriage)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"triage close-outs of issue %d: %w\", issue, err)\n+\t}\n+\tout := make([]Record, 0, len(jobs))\n+\tfor _, sj := range jobs {\n+\t\trec, err := decode(sj)\n+\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"triage close-outs of issue %d: %w\", issue, err)\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif rec.Job.ID == \"\" {\n+\t\t\trec.Job.ID = sj.ID\n \t\t}\n+\t\tout = append(out, rec)\n+\t}\n+\treturn out, nil\n+}\n+\n+// NetworkRoundOpen reports whether the newest bounced worker of issue has no\n+// triage engineer done after it (by job ID order, never created_at). doneRoles\n+// are the other triage roles that have a done close-out.\n+func NetworkRoundOpen(ctx context.Context, s closeoutsOf, issue int) (open bool, doneRoles []string, err error) {\n+\t_, bounced, err := networkRounds(ctx, s, issue)\n+\tif err != nil || bounced == \"\" {\n+\t\treturn false, nil, err\n+\t}\n+\trecs, err := triageRecords(ctx, s, issue)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn false, nil, err\n+\t}\n+\tseen := map[string]bool{}\n+\tfor _, rec := range recs {\n+\t\tif rec.Plan.Outcome != OutcomeDone {\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tswitch rec.Job.Role {\n+\t\tcase \"engineer\":\n+\t\t\tif rec.Job.ID > bounced {\n+\t\t\t\treturn false, nil, nil\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\tcase \"lead\":\n+\t\tdefault:\n+\t\t\tif !seen[rec.Job.Role] {\n+\t\t\t\tseen[rec.Job.Role] = true\n+\t\t\t\tdoneRoles = append(doneRoles, rec.Job.Role)\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\treturn true, doneRoles, nil\n+}\n+\n+// LeadRerun returns the roles the lead must re-open: the Rerun of the newest\n+// triage engineer record that re-armed the lead, unless a lead done was created\n+// after it.\n+func LeadRerun(ctx context.Context, s closeoutsOf, issue int) ([]string, error) {\n+\trecs, err := triageRecords(ctx, s, issue)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn nil, err\